The dead man had been saying one word since the day he died.

Nobody had understood him. He knew that now. It was the first thing he knew when he could know things again. Not the rope, or the cage, or the cold. He had been speaking the whole time, and what came out of him was not a word.

It came out as a moan.

--

He did not have eyes. He saw anyway. He had stopped wondering how.

The room was small and smelled of nothing, because he could not smell. He remembered that rooms like this smelled of bread. A woman slept in a chair by the window with her chin on her chest and flour on her sleeve. A candle had burned down to a puddle beside her.

The Bone Man lay in the bed.

The dead man knelt on the floorboards and held his hand. He had held it since the hill. He had held it while the gravedigger carried the Bone Man down, cursing the whole way, and he had held it up the stairs, and no one had told him to let go. The woman had looked at him once, for a long time. Then she had pulled the blanket up to the Bone Man's chin and sat down in the chair.

The hand was cold. The arm above it was gray to the elbow. Black veins ran under the skin like cracks in a plate.

The dead man looked at his own fingers, wrapped around the gray ones. Black bone. Black all the way through. The flesh had gone from him quickly on the gallows, and the bones underneath were already that color when the crows got down to them. He had burned from the inside out. That was why.

He knew that black. It was strange to see it on a living man.

But it was not the same. He knew that too, the way he knew the woman's sleeve was floured. The Bone Man was paying. A little, every time, out of his own body. The dead man had never paid for anything.

Then he had paid for all of it at once.

--

He had a name. He could not find it.

He could find other things. A plate. A cup. A girl with stringy hair who set them in front of him and did not ask for anything, and looked surprised every time he thanked her. He had thanked her every night for two months. He had liked watching her be surprised. He had thought that one day she would stop being surprised, and that would be a good day, and he would like to be there for it.

Her name he had. Nell.

He could find the seam, too. Black and tight and wet. He had been a middling wizard. He knew that about himself the way a man knows he is a middling singer. He cut coal, and the coal bit back. A patch of moss that screamed. A puddle that ran uphill. Once his own fingernails had turned to slate and stayed that way for a week.

Then he found the pebble.

It was in the coal face, low down, the size of a knuckle. Gray. Smooth. The dust did not stick to it. He had turned it over and over in the lamplight and then put it in his pocket, because it was pleasant to hold, and for no other reason.

After that, the coal stopped biting.

He did not think of the pebble. Why would he? He thought he had gotten good. He thought two months in Coalridge had made a wizard of him at last. He cut a week's coal in a day and nothing came back. Not a flower. Not a drop of blood. He cut more. The foreman clapped him on the shoulder. The Guild master learned his name.

He remembered that he had been happy. That was the worst thing to find.

And he remembered Bartholomew.

Bartholomew had been the first man in Coalridge to laugh at one of his jokes. Bartholomew strolled through the Pit with his thumbs in his belt and said wizards were only miners with worse luck. They ate together. They walked to the Flagon together, and Bartholomew elbowed him every time the girl set down his plate.

"What's that you're always rubbing?" Bartholomew had said. That last morning. In the seam.

He had shown him. Bartholomew tossed it from hand to hand. Held it to the lamp. Whistled.

"You'll get it back at supper," he said, and winked, and dropped it in his own pocket, and walked up the tunnel laughing.

It was a joke. It was only a joke.

The dead man had laughed too. Then he had turned to the coal face and done what he had done every day for a fortnight. He did not cut a seam. He cut the wall. All of it. The whole face, floor to roof, the way a man would who had forgotten that anything cost anything.

The wall came down clean.

Then the rest came.

He did not remember it as pain. He remembered it as heat, starting in the long bones of his legs and climbing. He remembered the lamp going red. He remembered being on his back, and boots running, and Bartholomew's face above him, upside down, with no joke left in it anywhere.

"I didn't know," Bartholomew said. "I didn't know. I didn't know."

His hand was pressed flat over his pocket.

The dead man had tried to speak then. One word. He had put everything he had left into it.

It did not come out as a word.

--

After that, the cart.

He remembered the quilt. Someone had tucked it around him, and he had known whose hands they were, and could not move to tell her so. He remembered her voice saying someone special, and breaking.

He remembered the quilt being pulled back. Two men did it. One of them was Bartholomew.

The pebble was in his pocket. The dead man could feel it. He felt it the way a tongue feels a tooth that isn't there. Ten feet away. Six. His fingers had moved toward it before the rest of him understood he was dead.

Someone gasped. Someone swore.

He said the word.

Then the rope went over the beam. Then the collar. Then the cage.

He said it again.

He said it every morning when the pit bell rang and the wizards crossed the square. He said it every evening when they crossed back. He said it in the rain. He said it at night, to the hut with the crooked corners, where a man lay awake and listened and did not understand.

That man had come out once, in the dark, and struck him with something that was not a fist. The dead man had not blamed him. It must have been a terrible thing to hear, night after night.

It was a terrible thing to say.

And every morning Bartholomew crossed the square with his thumbs in his belt and his eyes on the mud. He never looked at the gallows. Not once. Not in all those weeks.

That was all the dead man had wanted. He understood it now, kneeling on the floorboards with a cold hand in his. He had not wanted the pebble. He had not wanted anyone punished. He had only wanted his friend to look up.

--

Then the Bone Man had put a hook in him.

That was what it felt like. A hook behind the breastbone and a line running out of it, and at the other end a will that was not his. He had walked when it walked. He had stood when it stood. He had climbed the hill in the sun with the others and watched them lie down on their graves, one by one, and go to dust and light.

He had not gone to dust. He did not have a grave. He had only ever had a cage.

Then the Bone Man knelt in front of him, bleeding from the ear, and said the thing no one had said to him since he died.

Show me what you want.

He had not known. That was the truth. He had been saying one word for so long that he had forgotten there could be others. So he lifted his hand, because the Bone Man's hand was there, and the hook came out of him, and a voice said you're free, and he found that the first thing he wanted was to hold on to something.

He had been holding on ever since.

--

A chair scraped downstairs.

The dead man lifted his skull.

It was late. The pub was shut. The woman by the window had barred the door herself before she came up. But someone was down there by the fire. Someone she had not had the heart to turn out. He could hear a cup set down on wood. He could hear breathing, wet and uneven, the breathing of a man who has been drinking and has not found the bottom of it.

And under the breathing, through the floor, he felt it.

Small. Gray. Like a tooth that wasn't his.

He looked at the Bone Man. The chest rose and fell. The black veins did not move.

He laid the gray hand down on the blanket. He straightened the fingers, one at a time. Then he stood.

The woman in the chair opened her eyes.

She did not scream. She looked at him, standing there, black from skull to heel in the last of her candle. She looked at the hand he had laid so carefully on the blanket. Something moved in her face.

She closed her eyes again.

He went out.

--

There was a door at the top of the stairs. Someone slept behind it. He knew that breathing better than he knew his own name. He had listened to it through a quilt.

He stopped.

He lifted his hand to the wood.

He stood that way for a long time. Then he lowered it. She had buried him once. He would not make her do it again.

He went down.

The stairs did not creak. He weighed almost nothing now.

The fire had burned to coals. One lantern hung by the mantle. Bartholomew sat on a stool with his back to the stairs and a jug at his feet. His shoulders were shaking. He had the pebble in his fist. He was turning it over and over, the way the dead man used to.

"I saw where it came from," Bartholomew said to the fire. His voice was thick. "Today. I saw the rest of it. Standing in the wall with its arms out." He laughed, and it was a terrible sound. "Corven told me to break it. Me. And I thought, if I do, if it lets me, what comes due then? What comes due for all of us?"

He drank. He wiped his mouth.

"You'd have made a joke," he said. "You always had one. Better than mine."

The dead man stood at the foot of the stairs.

He said the word.

It came out the way it always had. Low. Broken. Not a word at all. The sound that had hung over the square every night for weeks while Coalridge pulled its blankets over its ears.

Bartholomew went still.

The cup slid out of his hand and rolled across the hearth. He did not turn around. His shoulders came up around his neck, the way a man's do when he is waiting to be hit.

The dead man waited. He had become very good at waiting.

Slowly, Bartholomew turned on the stool.

He looked. At the black feet. The black ribs. The black skull with the lantern light sliding over it. For the first time since the seam, he looked.

The dead man said it again.

Bartholomew's face came apart.

"Aye," he whispered. "Aye. That's me."

He held out his fist. It was shaking. He opened it. The pebble sat in his palm, gray and clean.

"Take it," he said. "Please. Take it back."

The dead man looked at it.

He lifted his own hand. Palm out. Fingers up.

Then he reached forward, and closed Bartholomew's fingers over the stone, one at a time, and let go.

He turned around. He climbed the stairs. He did not stop.

At the top, the door was open.

Nell stood in it with a blanket around her shoulders and her hair in her eyes. She had no candle. She did not need one. She looked at him the way she had looked at him every night for two months when he said thank you.

Surprised.

She put out her hand. It shook. She laid her palm on his skull.

Neither of them moved.

Then she turned and went back into her room. She left the door open behind her.

The dead man stood at the top of the stairs.

He did not say anything at all.
